s://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-417192176"},"message":"@cmaumet thanks for catching that. I sent out an update to the context and turtle docs in a separate email thread that got lost in the ether.\n\nThe updated context looks like:\n\n```\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"rdf\": \"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#\",\n    \"rdfs\": \"http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#\",\n    \"xsd\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#\",\n    \"dcterms\": \"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\",\n    \"name\": \"dcterms:name\",\n    \"nidm\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    },\n    \"spm\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/nidash/spm#\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    },\n    \"designMatrix\": \"nidm:designMatrix\",\n    \"software\": \"nidm:software\",\n    \"SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\": \"spm:spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\",\n    \"type\": {\n      \"@id\": \"rdf:type\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"#nidm-result\",\n  \"type\": \"nidm:Result\",\n  \"designMatrix\": {\n    \"type\": \"nidm:DesignMatrix\",\n    \"SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\": 128\n  },\n  \"software\": {\n    \"type\": \"nidm:Software\",\n    \"name\": \"SPM\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd the related turtle is:\n\n```\n\u003cfile:///Users/nicholb3/Downloads/#nidm-result\u003e a nidm:Result ;\n    nidm:designMatrix [ a nidm:DesignMatrix ;\n            spm:spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod 128 ] ;\n    nidm:software [ a nidm:Software ;\n            dcterms:name \"SPM\" ] .\n```\n\nThe _\"syntactic sugar\"_ provided by turtle makes this a bit misleading since the square brackets denote/imply a blank node. So, @cmaumet are you suggesting that a blank node references an additional blank node?\n\nFor a bit more clarity, it might help to spell this out w/o turtle and use the nquads/ntriples representation from the jsonld playground:\n\n```\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#software\u003e _:b1 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Result\u003e .\n_:b0 \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/spm#spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\u003e \"128\"^^\u003chttp://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer\u003e .\n_:b0 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#DesignMatrix\u003e .\n_:b1 \u003chttp://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/name\u003e \"SPM\" .\n_:b1 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Software\u003e .\n```\n\nI tried tinkering around by parsing examples that look like the using rapper that look like the example above:\n\n```\n@prefix dcterms: \u003chttp://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\u003e .\n@prefix nidm: \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#\u003e .\n_:a  [a \"nidm:Software\" ;\n            dcterms:name \"SPM\"]  .\n```\n\nBut it's not happy:\n\n```\n09:41:16 ~/Downloads$ rapper -i turtle -o ntriples foo.ttl\nrapper: Parsing URI file:///Users/nicholsn/Downloads/foo.ttlwith parser turtle\nrapper: Serializing with serializer ntriples\nrapper: Error - URI file:///Users/nicholsn/Downloads/foo.ttl:9 - syntax error, unexpected [, expecting a or URI literal or QName\nrapper: Failed to parse file FOO.TTL turtle content\nrapper: Parsing returned 0 triples\n```\n\n@jbpoline, the meaning of keywords in the \"@context\" like the example you provided (i.e., @id and @type) for the compacted version and their use in the flattened version have different roles to play. \n\nIn the @context, @id is used to declare that the key should be mapped to the specified URI and @type refers to the datatype of the value for that key, which can be a URI (i.e., @id) or a literal (e.g., xsd:string, xsd:date, etc.).\n\nIn the @graph portion, there is essentially a list of SPO triples where @id is the the key whose value is _subject_ and the remaining keys are the _predicates_ whose values are either literal or URI _objects_.\n\nHopefully that helps and isn't too confusing (json-ld is cool, but a bit complicated to understand and something I continue to struggle with so apologies if I missed something here).","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"7e6c03e441a9080012bf28ad8411faa34449a2c7"},"timestamp":1535606205,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEyNDcxODUxNg=="},"target":"a760c7a03cddc1851a850ac37c05fc04187cfcd67ae38e5cff713eda6a56e94a","message":"@cmaumet thanks for catching that. I sent out an update to the context and turtle docs in a separate email thread that got lost in the ether.\n\nThe updated context looks like:\n\n```\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"rdf\": \"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#\",\n    \"rdfs\": \"http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#\",\n    \"xsd\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#\",\n    \"dcterms\": \"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\",\n    \"name\": \"dcterms:name\",\n    \"nidm\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    },\n    \"spm\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/nidash/spm#\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    },\n    \"designMatrix\": \"nidm:designMatrix\",\n    \"software\": \"nidm:software\",\n    \"SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\": \"spm:spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\",\n    \"type\": {\n      \"@id\": \"rdf:type\",\n      \"@type\": \"@id\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"@id\": \"#nidm-result\",\n  \"type\": \"nidm:Result\",\n  \"designMatrix\": {\n    \"type\": \"nidm:DesignMatrix\",\n    \"SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\": 128\n  },\n  \"software\": {\n    \"type\": \"nidm:Software\",\n    \"name\": \"SPM\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nAnd the related turtle is:\n\n```\n\u003cfile:///Users/nicholb3/Downloads/#nidm-result\u003e a nidm:Result ;\n    nidm:designMatrix [ a nidm:DesignMatrix ;\n            spm:spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod 128 ] ;\n    nidm:software [ a nidm:Software ;\n            dcterms:name \"SPM\" ] .\n```\n\nThe _\"syntactic sugar\"_ provided by turtle makes this a bit misleading since the square brackets denote/imply a blank node. So, @cmaumet are you suggesting that a blank node references an additional blank node?\n\nFor a bit more clarity, it might help to spell this out w/o turtle and use the nquads/ntriples representation from the jsonld playground:\n\n```\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#software\u003e _:b1 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Result\u003e .\n_:b0 \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/spm#spm_SPMsDriftCutoffPeriod\u003e \"128\"^^\u003chttp://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer\u003e .\n_:b0 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#DesignMatrix\u003e .\n_:b1 \u003chttp://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/name\u003e \"SPM\" .\n_:b1 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Software\u003e .\n```\n\nI tried tinkering around by parsing examples using rapper that look like the example above:\n\n```\n@prefix dcterms: \u003chttp://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\u003e .\n@prefix nidm: \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#\u003e .\n_:a  [a \"nidm:Software\" ;\n            dcterms:name \"SPM\"]  .\n```\n\nBut it's not happy:\n\n```\n09:41:16 ~/Downloads$ rapper -i turtle -o ntriples foo.ttl\nrapper: Parsing URI file:///Users/nicholsn/Downloads/foo.ttlwith parser turtle\nrapper: Serializing with serializer ntriples\nrapper: Error - URI file:///Users/nicholsn/Downloads/foo.ttl:9 - syntax error, unexpected [, expecting a or URI literal or QName\nrapper: Failed to parse file FOO.TTL turtle content\nrapper: Parsing returned 0 triples\n```\n\n@jbpoline, the meaning of keywords in the \"@context\" like the example you provided (i.e., @id and @type) for the compacted version and their use in the flattened version have different roles to play. \n\nIn the @context, @id is used to declare that the key should be mapped to the specified URI and @type refers to the datatype of the value for that key, which can be a URI (i.e., @id) or a literal (e.g., xsd:string, xsd:date, etc.).\n\nIn the @graph portion, there is essentially a list of SPO triples where @id is the the key whose value is _subject_ and the remaining keys are the _predicates_ whose values are either literal or URI _objects_.\n\nHopefully that helps and isn't too confusing (json-ld is cool, but a bit complicated to understand and something I continue to struggle with so apologies if I missed something here).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1535617054,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNzIyMjg5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-417222892"},"message":"Thanks a lot @nicholsn! You are right, I had not interpreted correctly the squared brackets in turtle. Looking at N-quads is much more straightforward! \n\nI think we are close but the two first lines do not seem correct for NIDM-Results:\n```\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#software\u003e _:b1 .\n```\n\nHere `http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix` is used as an object property while it is defined as a class in our owl file. What we would like I think is:\n\n```\n_:b0 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#DesignMatrix\u003e  \u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e.\n_:b1 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Software\u003e \u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e.\n```\ni.e. to say that `_:b0` is a DesignMatrix and `_:b1` is a Software and that they both belong to the https://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result graph.\n\nAs far as I can see this can be done with @graph (cf. [here](https://json-ld.org/spec/latest/json-ld/#named-graphs)) but then we have an array and loose the nice dictionary that datalad (and other JSON applications) like... This brings me back to the [nested properties](https://json-ld.org/spec/latest/json-ld/#nested-properties). Is there another way around?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1535617054,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDEyNDc2OTczMQ=="},"target":"72ab0a752a720ea96bf5bdb8e35043388736827c0655892e422e43a3aed6dd35","message":"Thanks a lot @nicholsn! You are right, I had not interpreted correctly the squared brackets in turtle. Looking at N-quads is much more straightforward! \n\nI think we are close but the two first lines do not seem correct for NIDM-Results:\n```\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n\u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#software\u003e _:b1 .\n```\n\nHere `http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix` is used as an object property while it is defined as a class in our owl file. What we would like I think is:\n\n```\n_:b0 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#DesignMatrix\u003e  \u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e.\n_:b1 \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://purl.org/nidash/nidm#Software\u003e \u003chttps://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result\u003e.\n```\ni.e. to say that `_:b0` is a DesignMatrix and `_:b1` is a Software and that they both belong to the https://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result graph.\n\nAs far as I can see this can be done with @graph (cf. [here](https://json-ld.org/spec/latest/json-ld/#named-graphs)) but then we have an array and loose the nice dictionary that datalad (and other JSON applications) like... This brings me back to the [nested properties](https://json-ld.org/spec/latest/json-ld/#nested-properties). Can someone see another way around?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1535636721,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxNzMyNDc2NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-417324764"},"message":"ping @TomMaullin.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7e6c03e441a9080012bf28ad8411faa34449a2c7"},"timestamp":1536156336,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxODc0MzU1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-418743550"},"message":"Hi @cmaumet,\n\n\u003e Here http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#designMatrix is used as an object property while it is defined as a class in our owl file.\n\nRight, I was just making this up using _camelBack_ case to denote that `designMatrix` is a object property that links the document URI  (i.e., https://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result) to the actual instance of the type `DesignMatrix`.  This is the only way I could figure out using JSON-LD 1.0 that supports the datalad friendly standard json structure.\n\nNested properties look like a good way to go, but it isn't an immediate solution because the JSON-LD 1.1 spec isn't implemented for this feature yet in rdflib or other python tools.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1536312594,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQxOTM4MTA3NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-419381074"},"message":"\u003e Right, I was just making this up using camelBack case to denote that designMatrix is a object property that links the document URI (i.e., https://json-ld.org/playground/#nidm-result) to the actual instance of the type DesignMatrix. This is the only way I could figure out using JSON-LD 1.0 that supports the datalad friendly standard json structure.\n\nI see! We are on the same page. I like your proposed model but what I was hoping is to get an equivalent of our NIDM-Results RDF documents... Maybe we should wait for nested properties to be implemented... Anyway, thanks @nicholsn!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1536682543,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDMzMDk4Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-420330982"},"message":"Here is an update from the DC code convergence, w/ @jbpoline and @satra, using JSON-LD 1.1., we can have:\n\n```\n{\n  \"@context\": {\n    \"@version\": 1.1,\n    \"schema\": \"http://schema.org/\",\n    \"name\": \"schema:name\",\n    \"records\": {\n      \"@id\": \"@graph\",\n      \"@container\": \"@type\"\n    },\n    \"nidm\": \"http://nidm#\",\n    \"niiri\": \"http://niiri#\",\n    \"prov\": \"http://prov#\",\n    \"atLocation\": \"http://prov#atLocation\",\n    \"designMatrix\": \"nidm:designMatrix\",\n    \"data\": \"nidm:designMatrix\",\n    \"regressorNames\": \"nidm:regressorNames\"\n  },\n  \"name\": \"My NIDM graph\",\n  \"records\": {\n    \"designMatrix\": {\n      \"@id\": \"niiri:0001\",\n      \"name\": \"Some design matrix\",\n      \"atLocation\": \"/user/camaumet/DesignMatrix.csv\",\n      \"regressorNames\": \"[motor_left, motor_right]\"\n    },\n    \"data\": {\n      \"@id\": \"niiri:0002\",\n      \"name\": \"Some Data\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nwhich translates to Nquads:\n```\n\u003chttp://niiri#0001\u003e \u003chttp://nidm#regressorNames\u003e \"[motor_left, motor_right]\" _:b0 .\n\u003chttp://niiri#0001\u003e \u003chttp://prov#atLocation\u003e \"/user/camaumet/DesignMatrix.csv\" _:b0 .\n\u003chttp://niiri#0001\u003e \u003chttp://schema.org/name\u003e \"Some design matrix\" _:b0 .\n\u003chttp://niiri#0001\u003e \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n\u003chttp://niiri#0002\u003e \u003chttp://schema.org/name\u003e \"Some Data\" _:b0 .\n\u003chttp://niiri#0002\u003e \u003chttp://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type\u003e \u003chttp://nidm#designMatrix\u003e _:b0 .\n_:b0 \u003chttp://schema.org/name\u003e \"My NIDM graph\" .\n```\n\nThe key part is `\"records\": {\"@id\": \"@graph\", \"@container\": \"@type\"}` that let's us have \"records\" representing a named graph that is made of objects that are indexed by their types.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537690767,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzc5OTk1OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-423799958"},"message":"This looks great! It would be nice to have a full-sized demo metadata structure to implement a (close-to) real-world demo for metadata extraction, aggregation, and search witihin datalad (I would implement the latter, if I can have the former).\n\nFrom the POV of datalad, a JSON-LD 1.1 solution would be OK, because we would internally ignore this fact and should not suffer from absent support in rdflib. Moreover, in our dumb search we would probably (try to) detect a field being `{\"@id\": \"@graph\", \"@container\": \"@type\"}` and strip its wrapper-level from the actual metadata key structure. This would yield `nidmresults.designmatrix` instead of `nidmresults.records.designmatrix`.\n\nI wonder whether it would be possible (and better, as in more robust) to also fold the `name` property into the records object? This would prevent any \"accidental\" name space conflicts between inside and outside the `records` object. In a sense, it would also simplify the explanation of the desired structure. `records` would be the object-equivalent of an `@graph` array, thereby enabling direct, sort-order-independent, indexing of individual graph nodes.\n\nBut I guess that changes the semantics in an undesired way. So the records level either could not be removed in an expanded key (all levels chained), or we would need to require unique keys across all top-level properties and `record` keys.\n\nIn any case, given that this solution is not NIDM-specific (and even the name `records` is practically arbitrary), such a setup would also be a suitable recommendation for any metadata extractor where the desired metadata structure has yet to be determined.\n\nI am excited! Thx much!\n\nre JSON-LD 1.1 vs 1.0: Would it be possible to auto-convert the former into the latter with simple operations that do not require actual operations on an interpreted graph structure? If so, we could implement such a converter and maintain inter-operability with today's rdflib, without having to sacrifice the advantages of this metadata representation as the original format.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537691030,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMzgwMDE1OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-423800158"},"message":"For those interested in playing with the latest draft, here is a link to the JSON-LD playground with \nthe structure from https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-420330982: Playground http://tinyurl.com/ydxegkvd (this is using the dev version of the playground with support for JSON LD 1.1).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537861863,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNDI0MjE1OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-424242159"},"message":"[completely unrelated to the JSON-LD setup]\n\nI just noticed\n\n```\n\"atLocation\": \"/user/camaumet/DesignMatrix.csv\",\n```\n\nit would be better to use paths that are relative to a sensible anchor. For a datalad extractor that may be the dataset itself, for a NIDM pack it may be the pack location. The absolute path above will be invalid as soon as the metadata leaves the machine (or earlier). \n\nFor a datalad extractor we might want to look for this information and report that metadata snippet for the referenced file, not just as part of the dataset metadata, if it is not hidden in an archive.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"b6514b9b57c47fbb79fc9d0864ed8b5f63682d4e"},"timestamp":1537887458,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNDM3ODUxMA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-424378510"},"message":"@mih - i don't know if you saw this: https://github.com/satra/nidm-jsonld (which has a full example)\n\nregarding atLocation - it should indeed either be absolute on the web or relative to root document (nidm.ttl/jsonld).\n\nboth turtle and jsonld supports base iris, see details here: https://w3c.github.io/json-ld-syntax/#terminology","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1537895839,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNDQyNzg5NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-424427894"},"message":"Didn't see it yet, thx. But if RDFlib cannot handle JSON-LD 1.1, will this be the real thing?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"b6514b9b57c47fbb79fc9d0864ed8b5f63682d4e"},"timestamp":1537909072,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNDQ5OTE0OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-424499148"},"message":"@mih - don't know yet. we are debating whether to take over some of the maintenance, fork rdflib, etc.,. the current maintainers are quite busy it seems.\n\nalso json-ld 1.1 is still a draft spec.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538041422,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTAyODA5Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-425028093"},"message":"Quick update: With the changes in #2878 and the update in https://github.com/datalad/datalad-neuroimaging/pull/41 our default search (no whoosh, no index build, just a plain single pass through the metadata) can now do this:\n\n```\n% datalad -f json_pp \\\n  # next option needed to look for file results\n  -c datalad.search.index-egrep-documenttype=all \\\n  search \\\n    nidmresults.records.Agent:SPM \\\n    nidmresults.records.SoftwareAgent.softwareVersion:12b.5858 \\\n    nidmresults.records.ContrastMap:'passive listening'\n\n{\n  \"action\": \"search\",\n  \"dsid\": \"7a55f7a4-c237-11e8-b964-f0d5bf7b5561\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n  ...\n    \u003csnip the full metadata record with all details and the full context\u003e  \n  ...\n  }, \n  \"parentds\": \"/tmp/datalad_temp_test_nidm6yx7fm2d\",\n  \"path\": \"/tmp/datalad_temp_test_nidm6yx7fm2d/myfake.nidm.zip\",\n  \"query_matched\": {\n    \"nidmresults.records.Agent.rdfs-label\": \"SPM\",\n    \"nidmresults.records.ContrastMap.nidm-NIDM_0000085\": \"passive listening\",\n    \"nidmresults.records.ContrastMap.rdfs-label\": \"passive listening\",\n    \"nidmresults.records.SoftwareAgent.softwareVersion\": \"12b.5858\"\n  },\n  \"refcommit\": \"c8ff31326278b65052fbc1f7223d6ba468ce0e49\",\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"type\": \"file\"\n}\n```\n\nATM much information is still hidden inside the `Entity` array record (see https://github.com/datalad/datalad-neuroimaging/pull/41/files#diff-0356068541bfce90e22d75f2708943d3R373), hence inaccessible to the present implementation.\n\nDoes that look useful? What queries are worth testing?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1538041757,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTAyOTc3MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-425029770"},"message":"@mih: Thanks a lot! Sorry for not replying earlier. \n\n\u003e ATM much information is still hidden inside the Entity array record\n\nThis top `Entity` (same with `Activity`, `Agent`) will eventually be removed and replace by the most specific typed (e.g. `Peak`, `GrandMeanMap`, etc.). \n\nI'm working on automatically generating an example with this update. \n\n\u003e it would be better to use paths that are relative to a sensible anchor. For a datalad extractor that may be the dataset itself, for a NIDM pack it may be the pack location. \n\nAgreed. This is a bug of this manually crafted example. We do use relative paths in NIDM packs.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1538042070,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTAzMTI2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-425031267"},"message":"\u003e Sorry for not replying earlier.\n\n5 min delay is pretty good ;-)\n\n\u003e This top Entity (same with Activity, Agent) will eventually be removed and replace by the most specific typed (e.g. Peak, GrandMeanMap, etc.)\n\nThat sounds like the top-level keys would become more numerous, and the structure of the associated values less complex? That would be great from DataLad's perspective.\n\nPlease ping me when there is an update to the structure. I will update the test and report back.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1538142249,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyNTQzOTkzMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-425439933"},"message":"@mih: new examples with the removed extra \"Entity\" are in https://github.com/incf-nidash/nidm-specs/pull/479. \n\nFor example: [this one](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/incf-nidash/nidm-specs/8b48784a26039216eac669d1539511e04cea1c46/nidm/nidm-results/spm/spm_results.json)\n\nThere is still a bit of cleanup remaining but most of the conversion should be in place. Let me know if that works better with your tests! Thanks!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1539195854,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyODY3OTQzOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-428679438"},"message":"I have updated the test in the PR to match the latest example.\n\nSmall observation:\n\n```\ndatalad -f json_pp -c datalad.search.index-egrep-documenttype=all \\\n  search \\\n    nidmresults.records.prov-SoftwareAgent.rdfs-label:SPM \\\n    nidmresults.records.prov-SoftwareAgent.nidm-NIDM_0000122:12b.5853 \\ \n    nidmresults.records.ContrastWeightMatrix:'listening'\n```\n\nThe is the analog query to the example above. It would be good to be able to turn `nidm-NIDM_0000122` into `softwareVersion`, but the entry in the context looks like this:\n\n```\n    \"softwareVersion\": {\n      \"@id\": \"http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#NIDM_0000122\", \n      \"@type\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\"\n    }, \n```\n\nbut its use in the records is\n\n```\n    \"spm\": {\n      \"nidm:NIDM_0000122\": \"12b.5858\", \n    }\n```\n\nI think it would be as valid to turn the context into:\n\n```\n    \"softwareVersion\": {\n      \"@id\": \"nidm:NIDM_0000122\", \n      ...\n    }, \n```\n\nand then DataLad could look up such definitions and replace them with the context label.\n\nWhat do you think?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"b6514b9b57c47fbb79fc9d0864ed8b5f63682d4e"},"timestamp":1540299385,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzMjIzNTI1MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-432235251"},"message":"@mih - sorry for the long delay. i'm not completely following this:\n\nhere is the context entry (prior to @cmaumet recent context preparation)\nhttps://github.com/satra/nidm-jsonld/blob/master/nidm-results.jsonld#L1209\n\nhere is how it's represented\nhttps://github.com/satra/nidm-jsonld/blob/master/example.jsonld#L710\n\nin json-ld there is no difference between these two forms of the IRI:\n\n`http://purl.org/nidash/nidm#NIDM_0000122` and `nidm:NIDM_0000122`\n\nas long as the input document is in compact form (i.e., the output of applying context to the turtle graph) you should be able to refer to the key in your queries:\n\n`nidmresults.records.prov-SoftwareAgent.SoftwareVersion:12b.5853`","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1542032177,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzNzg5NjY0OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-437896648"},"message":"@satra: the document is in compact form. To me, the issue comes from the type definition `\"@type\": \"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string\"`: because `\"12b.5858\"` is not explicitly typed with `^^xsd::string`, the JSON-LD \"compactor\" do not recognize this attribute.\n\nThis also happens with booleans and object properties, cf. my comment [here](https://github.com/incf-nidash/nidm-specs/pull/479#issuecomment-425439499). \n\n@satra: this is something we should probably address on the NIDM side. If you can you add your thoughts directly here: https://github.com/incf-nidash/nidm-specs/pull/479 that would be great. Thanks!","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1556713409,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4ODI2OTY0Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-488269643"},"message":"FTR: I find the PROV-JSON approach relevant in the context of a general approach to metadata structures (and a verbalizable description of it).\n\nFrom http://www.w3.org/Submission/prov-json/\n\u003e A PROV document (see Document [PROV-N]) is used to wrap PROV assertions and facilitate their interchange. It is represented in PROV-JSON as a top-level JSON object; each type of PROV assertions (e.g. entity, activity, generation, usage, etc.) is organised into a separate property with the same name in the top-level object as follows.\n\u003e Each property itself is a JSON object, which is a map-like structure to hold all the PROV-JSON representation of assertions of the same type indexed by their identifiers. Hence, a PROV-JSON document is an indexed representation of a PROV document, in which PROV assertions are indexed by their types and by their identifiers. This allows fast look-up of specific assertions without the need to traverse the whole document.\n\nwhereas the following seems better approached via the JSON-*LD*-style context dict\n\u003e In addition, the top-level object contains an additional object called prefix that defines all the namespaces referred in the document. Prefixes can then be used to bind terms to specific namespaces. There is a special prefix called default, which can be used to declare the default namespace to which all unprefixed terms will be bound.\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"b6514b9b57c47fbb79fc9d0864ed8b5f63682d4e"},"timestamp":1556737384,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4ODM4MTMyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-488381325"},"message":"@mih - prov created the more recent prov-jsonld update as jsonld has matured. for mapping a graph model jsonld makes more practical sense than json.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"7c4c4bd9bc6cdb912a6d1bd757a509fa220d7bff"},"timestamp":1556778935,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4ODU2NjQ3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-488566475"},"message":"@mih - we had explored prov-json in the past but the development was stopped due to lack of libraries.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1556782012,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4ODU2ODU3Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-488568573"},"message":"@satra Thx for the pointer! Here is a paper on it https://nms.kcl.ac.uk/luc.moreau/papers/prov-jsonld-poster-abs.pdf The link to the referenced context is dead though: https://provenance.ecs.soton.ac.uk/prov.jsonld\n\n@cmaumet I was not actually advocating to use PROV-JSON, but I found the general approach to use objects, instead of arrays to be rather useful for working with the resulting metadata representation. However, I am still digesting. Cryptic object keys are also not very workable...","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1556782012,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDIxNzE0MzkzNA=="},"target":"f643500ceb718c490a8f1c088168f8dcf79f8ba33da3babdb0889cced311b24d","message":"@satra Thx for the pointer! Here is a paper on it https://nms.kcl.ac.uk/luc.moreau/papers/prov-jsonld-poster-abs.pdf The link to the referenced context is dead though: https://provenance.ecs.soton.ac.uk/prov.jsonld\n\nThis has all moved to: https://openprovenance.org (context is at https://openprovenance.org/prov.jsonld)\n\n@cmaumet I was not actually advocating to use PROV-JSON, but I found the general approach to use objects, instead of arrays to be rather useful for working with the resulting metadata representation. However, I am still digesting. Cryptic object keys are also not very workable...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"b6514b9b57c47fbb79fc9d0864ed8b5f63682d4e"},"timestamp":1556797728,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQ4ODY0Mjc5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753#issuecomment-488642795"},"message":"@cmaumet - the new link is: https://openprovenance.org/prov.jsonld - but it's a very minimal schema. you'll probably need to extend it for almost anything nidm or otherwise.\n\n@mih - the activity/activity-set jsonld files in reproschema may be more useful to wrap around some of the new concepts in both jsonld 1.1 and how to think like a graph.\n\nhere is a raw jsonld schema + context file: https://github.com/ReproNim/schema-standardization/tree/master/activity-sets/voice-pilot\n\nand visualized on playground: https://tinyurl.com/y3hzxm7e","files":null}]}